From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: English spelling

Jeff Fabijanic writes:

>I suspect that the English-speaking world's predilection for adopting
>"foreign" words and the various spelling conventions that come with them,
>is one of the reasons for its incredible success. I agree with those who say
>that English can be confusing, contradictory, and irrational, but I believe
>that's exactly what allows it to be subtle, surprising and profound as well.

Actually, most "foreign" words in English came from French, via the Norman 
invasion in 1066. Also, the Great Vowel Shift, which happened around the 14th 
century (and which is why Chaucer sounds so weird when you read him correctly) 
radically changed the way the language sounded, but the spelling system was not 
modified to reflect the changes.  We are speaking 20th-Century English but 
spelling it with 13th-century orthography.  Loan-words coming to the language 
from other sources (e.g., Latin and Greek in science) have much less to do with 
the bizarreness of the system.

As for English being "subtle, surprising, and profound", I can't see how that 
has anything to do with inconsistency, except of course for writers who exploit 
the inconsistency in a humorous way.  Nor do I believe that English is any more 
subtle, surprising, or profound than any other tongue.  Shakespeare would have 
been brilliant in any language; we're just lucky he was born in England (and 
unlucky that Pushkin was born in Russia, Moliere in France, Goethe in Germany, 
Dante in Italy...).

Finally, willingness to borrow from other languages has nothing to do with 
spelling; the Japanese are notorious for borrowing heavily (esp. from English), 
but they spell all loan-words using their katakana syllabary.

